<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # So Many Different Historical Events to Learn! – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #333; margin-top: 5px; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Learning to Research History</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> So Many Different Historical Events to Learn!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze informational content for specific data. Reading: Summarize narratives of significant events, recent or historical.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can identify key details and perspectives in a text about Panama's history (e.g., "What year did Panama declare independence, and how is this event described in different texts?"). Can compare the main events of Panama's history as presented in two different narratives (e.g., "How does this source describe the canal's construction compared to a second source?"). Can evaluate a historical timeline by explaining the significance of specific events in context (e.g., "Why was the completion of the Panama Canal in 1914 a pivotal event in global trade?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f0f8ff;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images related to different historical events in Panama (e.g., a picture of old Panama City, a map of the canal, a flag from independence). Students work in pairs to identify the events and discuss what they already know about them for 3 minutes. The teacher prompts: "What historical events do you recognize? Why are they significant?" Students use vocabulary like 'independence', 'canal', 'economy', 'culture', 'heritage'.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a key vocabulary word from the previous lesson, like "independence," on the board and asks students to recall its meaning. Then, the teacher models reading aloud a short sentence using the past simple, e.g., "Panama <b>declared</b> its <b>independence</b> in 1903." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of "-ed" in 'declared' (/d/) and the stress in 'independence'. The teacher then introduces the concept of analyzing texts for "specific data" and "summarizing narratives," modeling how to quickly scan a short paragraph to find a date or a main idea.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I ask you to 'analyze informational content for specific data,' what should you look for in a text?" (Expected answer: Dates, names, places, numbers, key facts).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When you 'summarize a narrative,' do you include every small detail or just the main points?" (Expected answer: Just the main points or key events).</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Learning to Research History".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a short informational text titled "Panama: A Nation Shaped by History" (approx. 250 words). The text is an excerpt from a historical article, describing key historical events such as Panama's separation from Colombia, the construction of the Panama Canal, and its transfer back to Panama, emphasizing their 'significance' and 'legacy'. It contains targeted vocabulary (e.g., <i>history, Panama, independence, separation, canal, significance, construction, economy, events, transformation, legacy</i>) and grammar (past simple, passive voice: "The canal <i>was completed</i> in 1914," and reported speech: "Historians <i>say</i> the canal <i>transformed</i> Panama's economy").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret informational texts related to historical events, understanding main ideas and specific details.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (First Reading - Gist):</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the text individually.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is the main topic of the article?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What two major historical events in Panama does the article highlight?</li>
                    </ol>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice identifying specific data, understanding historical narratives, and recognizing past simple, passive voice, and reported speech in context, using target vocabulary (<i>declared, completed, transformed, significance, economy, legacy</i>).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - True/False & Evidence):</b> Students re-read the text carefully. The teacher provides 5-6 True/False statements about the text. Students must correct the false statements and cite the line or phrase from the text that supports their answer. Example statement: "Panama's <b>separation</b> from Colombia occurred after the canal <b>was completed</b>." (False: it was before).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Grammar in Context):</b> The teacher instructs students to identify and underline all sentences in the text that use the <b>passive voice</b> and <b>reported speech</b>. Then, in pairs, they discuss why these grammatical structures are used (e.g., passive voice to focus on the event rather than the agent, reported speech to attribute information). For example, finding "The canal <i>was completed</i> in 1914" and "Historians <i>reported</i> that this event <i>transformed</i> global trade." They also highlight 5-6 vocabulary words (e.g., <i>diversity, identity, revolution</i>) and discuss their meaning in the context of the text. The teacher ensures correct pronunciation of -ed endings (e.g., /d/ in 'improved', /t/ in 'accomplished', /ɪd/ in 'suggested') and two-syllable nouns/verbs like 'research'.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction - Information Gap Reading):</b> Students are given a second, shorter paragraph about another related historical event (e.g., the expansion of the canal or a specific cultural period), containing similar vocabulary and grammatical structures. In pairs, Student A reads text 1 and Student B reads text 2. They then "report" the main facts and key events to each other, using reported speech (e.g., "My text says that the Panama Canal's expansion <i>began</i> in 2007...") and practice asking about important historical events ("When did Panama <b>separate</b> from Colombia?" or "What impact did the construction of the Panama Canal have on the country's <b>economy</b>?").</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-20 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Learning to Research History".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in groups of three. Each group receives two short, different historical narratives (e.g., one on the U.S. involvement in the Canal's construction, another on Panama's struggle for sovereignty over the Canal Zone). The texts will incorporate vocabulary (<i>revolution, transformation, legacy, heritage</i>) and grammar (past simple, passive voice). Their task is to <b>compare</b> the main events and perspectives presented in the two narratives and create a mini-timeline (3-4 key points) summarizing the combined information. They must use specific dates and details from the texts. Finally, each group will present their timeline and briefly explain the <b>significance</b> of one event, engaging in group discussions to summarize Panama's historical events. Example: "<i>Panama's separation from Colombia in 1903 was a turning point because it led to the canal's construction, which boosted global trade.</i>" They practice pronunciation of stress for two-syllable nouns and verbs like "research" (noun stress on first syllable, verb stress on second).</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and presentation, focusing on students' ability to identify key details, compare narratives, summarize events, and use target language. Peer assessment during presentations will also be used.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their group's mini-timeline and a short (3-4 sentences) written summary comparing the main events from the two narratives, explicitly identifying differences in perspective or additional details. This demonstrates their ability to "analyze informational content for specific data" and "summarize narratives of significant events."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and accuracy in identifying key historical details (dates, events, people).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective comparison of information from different sources.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>significance, transformation, legacy</i>) and grammar (past simple, passive voice, reported speech).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of key vocabulary when presenting.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and engagement in group discussions.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of comparing the two historical narratives?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new historical fact about Panama I learned today is..."</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did identifying specific data help you understand the overall significance of the historical events?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What strategies did you use today to summarize the narratives effectively?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The reading comprehension activities worked well, and students were able to identify specific data. The information gap activity helped reinforce summarizing and comparing. Some students struggled with differentiating between the use of reported speech and direct quotes, so more focused practice on this grammar point might be needed. Additionally, a few students found it challenging to evaluate the 'significance' of events beyond just listing them. I will plan to provide more sentence starters for expressing opinions and analyzing impact in future lessons.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on reading and understanding historical texts. In the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction), students will use the historical facts, vocabulary, and grammatical structures (past simple, reported speech, passive voice) they learned today to engage in active discussions and debates about the impact and legacy of these historical events on Panama's identity and culture.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>